00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:33.270
GANG CHEN: OK, a recap
of our last lecture--

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.580
there are many, many,
many terminologies

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:36.730
in the last lecture.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:41.700
We basically start from
the Boltzmann equation

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.360
under relaxation
time approximation.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.870
And we derived the expression
for the charge flux,

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.010
current flux, which
current density, and also

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:01.540
for the heat carried
by the charge.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.080
And so we have the
coupled equation

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.090
for electrical transport.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.540
I have to, like I
point out before, I use

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.840
the different symbol Je and JQ.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.980
Je is for electron, JQ
in the book for heat.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:23.250
And always the challenge is
to deal with the negative sign

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.490
of electron.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.530
So I put a q, small q, just
for whatever is positive

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:32.880
is for whole, negative,
that's for electrons.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.380
And then I used a capital Q.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:37.540
So that's what I did
in the last lecture.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.460
And so we have those
coupled equations.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:47.610
And those type of
coupling is very common

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:54.090
when you deal with say
the transport processes.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.820
Here we do the electron
and the heat coupling.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.520
I said when you do piezoelectric
and heat and mass transfer,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.090
there are all those
type of relations.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.520
And if you generalize
the gradient

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.780
by the generalized entropy
gradient as the force,

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.190
then you have the Onsager
relation between those coupling

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:18.240
coefficients.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.790
In the generalized form,
those coupling coefficients

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.510
are equal to each other.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.260
But since we're not
writing into the entropy,

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.870
we're writing
temperature, so L 2y

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:35.800
equals T L1 2 in this
specific expression we use.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:42.190
And from this, it's natural to
say when you have a temperature

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:46.780
gradient, you also have,
even in the open circuit

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.680
case, or any conductor
or semiconductor,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.200
you have the Seebeck,
you have voltage

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.540
generated if you have
a temperature gradient.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.220
And the Peltier
effect is when you

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.220
have a charge flow
in the conductor,

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.720
even if you don't
have a temperature

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.350
gradient, associated with
the charge flow is the heat

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.750
current, is the heat flow.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.490
And that's the Peltier heat,
which has manifested itself

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:18.850
when you put two material
together and form a junction.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:23.080
And because of discontinuity
in this Peltier coefficient,

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.360
you have cooling or
heating at the junction.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.480
But fundamentally,
it's an effect that

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.630
happens in the same material.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:35.080
So we talk about the
material properties,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:39.310
Seebeck coefficient,
Peltier coefficient material

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.480
properties.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:44.350
And similarly,
Thompson effect is

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.990
the Seebeck and
Peltier were actually

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:50.140
experimentally discovered.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.080
But Thompson effect
is Lord Kelvin

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.160
predicted when he tried
to unify and then give us

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.040
a general interpretation
of what's a Seebeck

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.780
and what's Peltier effect.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.020
He discovered there has
to be a third effect.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.640
That's when you
have a temperature

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.880
gradient and the current flow at
the same time in the material,

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.830
then you have distributed
local cooling and the heating

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:18.529
along every point
in the material.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:21.240
So the relation between
those coefficients

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.450
are Kelvin relations.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:25.510
And you can see there
are a lot of relations.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:30.280
That's because all those
expression, flux expression,

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.720
is derived at least under
the framework we're talking,

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.790
the relaxation
time approximation.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.700
You don't have to use that.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:41.240
But eventually
those coefficients

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:45.760
are all related back
into the relaxation time.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.770
So we have say the relation
between electric conductivity

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:54.340
and thermal conductivity for
charge, Wiedemann-Franz law,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.910
we have relationship between
Peltier and Seebeck effect,

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.560
that the Kelvin relation.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:04.330
We also have in the
electrical engineering,

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.440
say the drift-diffusion
equation,

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:11.710
which is actually just
the first term of the JQ.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.600
L1 1 is the electric
conductivity.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.790
And then when you run into
the separate expression.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.240
When we split out
the driving force

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.920
for the current flow, which
is electrochemical potential,

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.220
that includes the
chemical potential

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.500
and electrostatic potential.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.780
The chemical potential is
related to the carrier density.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:36.880
So that's a gradient.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.590
That's a diffusion term.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.960
And electrostatic
potential gives you

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.200
the force, electrical
force, acting on the charge.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.270
So that drives the drift.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:50.440
So drift is a measure
of the mobility.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.750
Diffusion is a diffusivity.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:57.760
And again, the relation between
diffusivity and mobility

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.460
is the Einstein relation.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.560
And in fact, that
Einstein relationship--

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.320
I made a comment that
Einstein didn't do electron,

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:09.430
he did the Browning
motion for molecules.

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:15.070
And he has relation between
the mass diffusivity

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:16.690
and the viscosity.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.300
And that's a special case.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:24.490
All those are special case of
the fluctuation dissipation

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:25.330
theory.

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:30.100
So the fluctuation gives the
Browning motion diffusion.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.440
And dissipation
is the viscosity.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.410
Mobility, that's
the correlation.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:39.340
So all things are tied together.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:46.270
A lot of terminology, but the
are intrinsically connected.

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:49.780
And by the end of
the lecture, we

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:53.620
went to discuss the
thermoelectric device.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:07:02.410
A real device is typically
made of pairs of P and N.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:07.450
And then you connect
them electrically by--

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:10.180
typically it's a metal.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:13.750
And then say this is the
basic construction unit.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:20.630
And you repeat them many
times in a commercial device.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:25.000
And the reason for this,
you connect the many of them

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.780
together, is because
the typical ZT,

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.750
similar as a electric
figure of merit.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.750
When you get a ZT,
the largest case

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.995
in commercial material, which
is bismuth telluride base.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.450
Right the Seebeck
is of the order

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:48.370
of 200 microwatt per Kelvin.

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:51.520
And the electric
conductivity is of the 10

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.700
to the 5th ohm per meter.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.830
And the ohm per meter,
inverse ohm is also siemens.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:03.730
So it is siemens per
meter, or siemens

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:07.000
per meter, different unit.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.520
So it 10 to the 5th,
so the materials

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.440
are actually very conductive.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:17.200
Metals, electric conductivity
is 10 to the 8th of the order.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.000
Yeah, I think it's order--

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:25.120
two or three order magnitude
higher than this semiconductor.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.780
Typically we use as a
common doping concentration

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:32.080
is around 10 to the 19th.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.950
And if you make a silicon
device in microelectronics,

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:38.020
the doping concentration
is typically 10

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:41.870
to the 17th, so two order
of magnitude smaller.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:45.700
So this is-- each leg
is about a millimeter

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.140
in commercial device.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:51.480
And the resistance electrical
resistance is a milliohm.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.700
So we have milliohm,
you've got a large current.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:59.510
And so you put a
lot of legs together

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:04.460
to increase the electrical
resistance and that way you

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.420
your operation is easier.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:10.130
You don't have to have that
large current operation.

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:15.740
And the thermal conductivity is
made of electronic contribution

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.670
and the phonon contribution.

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:23.060
And this is of the order
of 1 watt meter Kelvin.

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:29.120
And this value is
comparable to glass.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:33.520
Glass is 1.2, and
this is a similar--

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:36.860
typical material is
actually about 1.5.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:38.420
And so you want
the material with

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.620
the high electric conductivity,
no thermal conductivity.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:44.710
And you want the electron
carry a lot of heat.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:50.110
Seebeck is the entropy, average
entropy carried per charge

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:51.520
I mentioned.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:52.960
So that's the dilemma there.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.660
You have to play
with the material.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.550
So you try to--

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:01.540
it turns out that you change
one, you also most of the time

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:04.050
are changing the others.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:08.300
And there is a nice terminology
to describe what you really

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:09.140
want.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:13.370
You want-- this is a
so-called phonon glass.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:15.770
So for lattice
vibration, you want it

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:19.490
like a glass,
which is amorphous.

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:21.560
And amorphous material
typically do not

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:24.005
conduct electricity well.

00:10:24.005 --> 00:10:25.380
So you want the
electron crystal.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:31.290
So phonon glass,
electron crystal, but you

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:33.980
want this happening
in the same material.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:36.860
So this is where
the challenge is.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.620
And they're trying to fool
the material for the lattice

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:43.310
vibration, you say this is like
a very poor heat conductor.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:46.700
And for electron, is like
a good electric conductor.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.460
And typically,
bismuth telluride,

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:52.780
why it's good it's
because it's heavy.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:57.250
And when it's heavy, the
phonon frequency is low.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.910
The group velocity is small.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.120
And intrinsically, the
thermal conductivity

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.800
is going to be lower.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.520
And the other way to use it
to reduce thermal conductivity

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:09.500
is by alloy.

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:13.040
The mass scatter, for example,
silicon, thermal conductivity

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:16.070
150, germanium is about 90.

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.100
And then when you mix
the atoms together,

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:20.840
the difference of
the mass now, if you

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.750
think about having a chain,
of each atom is the same,

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.810
you got a harmonic
vibration, very harmonic.

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:29.450
But each mass point, you
put a different mass,

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:32.600
you know that string is not
going to vibrate very well.

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:35.780
And that's a mass difference
scattering, alloys scattering.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.270
And that's the
typical way to reduce

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:40.040
the thermal conductivity.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:43.310
And of course, you want
to do it not to reduce

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:46.250
the electrical conductivity.

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:51.680
So we discussed some of
the typical characteristic

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:54.410
of s sigma k.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:12:00.170
And if you engineer-- or say
most of you are engineers--

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:03.530
so what's important
is to match--

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:06.210
it turns out the
device you can size

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.840
the different
cross-section area, height.

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:11.840
And you actually have
to match the two sides,

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:14.930
because the analysis
I gave is just 1 leg.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:16.520
When you put the
two leg together,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:20.210
you'll see area, length ratio
has to be matched together,

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:22.200
maximum performance.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.200
So there are a lot
of optimization

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:31.260
at both materials level and
also at the device level.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.740
OK, any questions?

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:41.100
So if you look at the
energy research, and right

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.660
now there are a lot of--

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:47.100
of course of great interest,
and a lot of energy devices.

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.590
There are not many stuff, right?

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:52.850
A lot of people
work on solar cells.

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:56.300
A lot of people
work on batteries.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:01.500
And the rest is--

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:05.692
you just say people all want
to work on say energy related,

00:13:05.692 --> 00:13:07.150
so you suddenly
see a lot of people

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:09.260
now work on thermoelectrics.

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:11.500
Right but actually
thermoelectrics

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:18.060
are fun in the sense you really
have to fool the material.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:22.590
And it goes into a
non-linear regime.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:24.810
Everything is scattering.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:26.490
You want to control
transport, you

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:27.810
have to control the scatter.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:33.170
So it's both difficult, and
if you like it, it's fun.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.270
OK, don't like it,
it's a headache.

00:13:35.270 --> 00:13:38.450
When I got into thermoelectrics,
the first thermoelectric

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.480
program--

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:45.360
so if you look at
history, in 1821

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.880
Seebeck discovered the effect.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:54.810
Around 1900, this parameter
ZT was formulated.

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:58.470
So how you make a
generator, you need

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:01.170
a material with the high ZT.

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:08.510
And around 1950, bismuth
telluride was discovered.

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:11.620
And that's when so
this was by Goldsmid.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:14.260
And when he discovered
bismuth telluride,

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.630
he said that he was just
actually very lucky.

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:20.260
He said the first
guess he found it.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:23.470
Here's a good physical insight,
said you need a heavy material.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.560
Heavy material turns out
are also usually have

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:30.850
better mobility,
charge mobility.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:35.030
And so he tried bismuth
telluride and it worked.

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:42.210
And so most commercial device
was based on bismuth telluride.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:48.930
And then say for
power generation NASA

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.860
was interested because
the solid state.

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:53.330
And it's based
on-- but they have

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:54.580
to go to a higher temperature.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:57.000
Bismuth telluride is not stable.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.120
So they went to a silicon.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:02.810
Silicon germanium is
by the idea of alloy,

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:08.090
that's the Russian scientist
Ioffe proposed alloy can

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:09.890
reduce thermal conductivity.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:14.030
So that led to develop
a silicon germanium.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:18.380
And silicon germanium has
been used the most NASA flight

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:20.330
for deep space missions.

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:23.180
And they use a nuclear waste--

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:28.170
not nuclear waste-- nuclear
fuel to generate the temperature

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.150
difference, generate heat.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.540
And that has turned out that
for space it is very reliable.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:39.540
And those generator work,
not very efficient, 5% to 6%.

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:43.560
But they've been sending back
signals, some of those space

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:46.920
missions, over 30 years.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:53.010
And for cooling, has been
based on bismuth telluride,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:56.280
terrestrial applications
based on bismuth telluride.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:59.820
So commercial modules
all use the pretty much

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:03.380
the mature developed
in the '50s and '60s.

