because I say if you go to
look at the Boltzmann equation,

00:34:23.139 --> 00:34:26.690
here, right, you go to here.

00:34:26.690 --> 00:34:30.580
In fact, I say you'll find
that you can transform you

00:34:30.580 --> 00:34:32.650
put a tau onto the other side--

00:34:32.650 --> 00:34:36.400
oh, here should be
a tau, otherwise

00:34:36.400 --> 00:34:38.949
the unit is not right.

00:34:38.949 --> 00:34:41.800
So you put a tau on this side.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:45.683
You integrate times of
velocity times the h omega

00:34:45.683 --> 00:34:46.850
times the density of states.

00:34:46.850 --> 00:34:50.199
So you integrate both sides.

00:34:50.199 --> 00:34:53.050
You will actually
get this term, you

00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:56.380
get this term, that's
this term here.

00:34:56.380 --> 00:35:01.010
And it's this term, you
approximate it still

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:02.344
with the Fourier law.

00:35:04.930 --> 00:35:08.800
So I say this is the most of
the time as academic exercise

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:13.590
because when you have very
fast temperature process,

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:16.700
your gradient,
the gradient term,

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:19.340
the spatial gradient
that typically exists,

00:35:19.340 --> 00:35:24.830
and because of spatial gradient,
you cannot approximate this

00:35:24.830 --> 00:35:30.710
term by the Fourier law
because we're doing--

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:33.680
we're replacing that
gradient f by f0.

00:35:36.640 --> 00:35:39.040
So this is what I say.

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:44.320
There are many PhD
statements written on this.

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:46.970
And say I say that's a waste
of time, unfortunately.

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:50.230
And you have to really
understand what you're

00:35:50.230 --> 00:35:52.390
doing when you deal with those.

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:59.470
So comment, that's
the one comment.

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:01.150
The next comment
I want to make--

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:03.760
I'm not going to
go into the detail.

00:36:03.760 --> 00:36:05.260
It's all in the book.

00:36:05.260 --> 00:36:07.330
Very interesting, you can read.

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:12.430
So what we have derived is
a constitutive relation.

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:14.710
The relation between
temperature gradient,

00:36:14.710 --> 00:36:19.840
the heat flux, velocity
gradient, shear stress,

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:22.570
electrochemical potential
gradient current,

00:36:22.570 --> 00:36:25.270
that's constitutive relation.

00:36:25.270 --> 00:36:30.870
And you can actually start
with Boltzmann equation, derive

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:33.810
conservation equations.

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:48.520
And so if you recall when
you derive the Navier-Stokes

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:52.230
equation for fluid flow,
what do you use before,

00:36:52.230 --> 00:36:53.970
or you derive heat
conduction equation,

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:56.340
you use the
constitutive relation

00:36:56.340 --> 00:37:00.760
together with the energy and
momentum balance conservation

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:01.740
equations.

00:37:01.740 --> 00:37:04.930
That's how you derive
those equations, right?

00:37:04.930 --> 00:37:08.950
And you can start from
the Boltzmann equation

00:37:08.950 --> 00:37:12.480
and you take the moment.

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:17.490
f is the distribution
function in phase space.

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:24.690
So if you multiply by the mass
per particle on both sides

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:27.300
and then you integrate,
you have to do

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:33.870
a summation of all the
states on both sides.

00:37:33.870 --> 00:37:35.790
You will get mass
conservation equation.

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:42.090
And if you do--

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.910
so this is per
particle, because when

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:48.420
we do all the Navier-Stokes
equation or heat conduction

00:37:48.420 --> 00:37:54.030
equation, we do not have any
variable in the momentum space.

00:37:54.030 --> 00:37:55.530
It's a real space variable.

00:37:55.530 --> 00:38:01.020
So when we sum up all
the momentum variable,

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:03.450
you're taking the
average on both sides

00:38:03.450 --> 00:38:09.530
of the mass, total mass,
any location there.

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:11.920
And if you do your
momentum, you put

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:20.330
in the momentum per particle
into the Boltzmann equation,

00:38:20.330 --> 00:38:23.200
and again your
average both sides,

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:25.310
you will get at
the left hand side,

00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:30.380
you will have the convection
term in the Navier-Stokes

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:32.680
equation.

00:38:32.680 --> 00:38:38.990
On the right hand side,
you'll get the right hand

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:42.440
side of Navier-Stokes
pressure and the newton shear

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:45.860
stress, the shear stress
term, viscose term.

00:38:45.860 --> 00:38:52.380
But there you still have to put
in the constitutive relation

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:53.800
to get the Newton shear stress.

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:58.440
So you say here, you still
are using the approximation.

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:01.390
Navier-Stokes equation is
approximation because of you

00:39:01.390 --> 00:39:05.140
approximate the Newton
shear stress term.

00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:07.520
As same you can do energy.

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:12.620
So on this side, say the
fluids has more higher order,

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:16.190
you can do higher order moments
and there are modifications

00:39:16.190 --> 00:39:19.360
to Navier-Stokes equations.

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:21.040
But what's interesting
is that you

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:25.950
go to look at the history
of electrical engineering.

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:29.670
And in the 1980s, the
electrical engineer

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:32.900
started doing sims
then and they derived

00:39:32.900 --> 00:39:37.980
the electohydrodynamic equation
for electron transport.

00:39:37.980 --> 00:39:43.440
So you go to check
the derivation,

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:46.670
I think in my book
their reference to this,

00:39:46.670 --> 00:39:49.410
you check the derivation.

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:51.330
So in '98, in the
literature '98,

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:53.820
they did a lot of
paper trying to solve

00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:57.280
electrohydrodynamic equation.

00:39:57.280 --> 00:40:04.360
And the reason for that is
at the time the gate length

00:40:04.360 --> 00:40:05.530
is becoming smaller.

00:40:05.530 --> 00:40:07.790
And people were worried
the size effects.

00:40:07.790 --> 00:40:10.850
So they went to
electrohydrodynamic equation.

00:40:10.850 --> 00:40:12.530
And of course, if
you look back, if I

00:40:12.530 --> 00:40:20.430
say that seems to be really not
the right approach because we

00:40:20.430 --> 00:40:23.160
know the Navier-Stokes equation
is not valid when the mainframe

00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:28.650
path is say longer than
they carriers can transport,

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:30.330
dimension, right?

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:33.150
So when you do
hydrodynamic, you're

00:40:33.150 --> 00:40:34.800
not solving that problem.

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:37.480
You're still doing
the local equilibrium.

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:40.380
And of course the high
field, there is a convection.

00:40:40.380 --> 00:40:43.070
So those things may
say how the electron

00:40:43.070 --> 00:40:45.850
effect may be captured.

00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:50.320
So I do not think
there are really

00:40:50.320 --> 00:40:54.740
many people nowadays solving
electrohydrodynamic equations.

00:40:54.740 --> 00:40:57.310
But even you go
to check, there is

00:40:57.310 --> 00:41:00.790
electron shear stress, electron
temperature, very fuzzy,

00:41:00.790 --> 00:41:05.560
the discussion there were
very fuzzy in the literature.

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:10.735
And then you go to
phonon, there are also

00:41:10.735 --> 00:41:13.840
phonon hydrodynamic equation.

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:16.720
These were in
Russian literature.

00:41:16.720 --> 00:41:20.230
And nobody has solved it.

00:41:20.230 --> 00:41:22.420
I haven't solved any solution.

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:24.220
So you see there is--

00:41:24.220 --> 00:41:27.290
the point that it's
all related, right?

00:41:27.290 --> 00:41:31.920
And different fields that
has probably some field

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:33.410
developed tools.

00:41:33.410 --> 00:41:38.600
And the other people learn and
apply to different problems.

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:41.480
But see, you got to
understand that what are

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:46.360
the basis when you apply it.

00:41:46.360 --> 00:41:50.260
So that's all on
this chapter six,

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:55.130
which is really starting
from the particle transport.

00:41:55.130 --> 00:41:59.490
Then we learned the
diffusion theory.

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:04.020
And what we're moving on is
looking into classic or size

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:05.565
effect, that's Chapter 7.

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:21.120
OK, so now we get into--

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:25.350
want to look at the regime when
those approximations are not

00:42:25.350 --> 00:42:27.960
valid.

00:42:27.960 --> 00:42:30.330
And we can start
with the problem

00:42:30.330 --> 00:42:32.820
where I'm looking at
the heat conduction.

00:42:32.820 --> 00:42:37.620
I do heat transfer now, heat and
conduction along a thin film,

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:40.570
freestanding thin film.

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:44.200
OK, and let's say
the mean free path--

00:42:44.200 --> 00:42:49.960
we know the mean free path of
phonons and the mean free path

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:53.944
is longer than they
film thickness, d.

00:42:58.340 --> 00:43:01.490
Now even though I
have-- let me first

00:43:01.490 --> 00:43:04.460
consider the transport
along x direction.

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:06.830
So my q is flowing
along the film.

00:43:09.910 --> 00:43:13.250
And even though the q is
flowing along the film,

00:43:13.250 --> 00:43:16.950
we know the phonons
go all directions.

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:22.620
So phonons, the
momentum space, if it

00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:26.580
goes this direction
and the mean free path

00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:29.970
is longer than the
distance I draw here,

00:43:29.970 --> 00:43:33.490
you will collide
with the boundary.

00:43:33.490 --> 00:43:36.670
And of course once
collided with the boundary,

00:43:36.670 --> 00:43:41.800
they will be
scattered, reflected.

00:43:41.800 --> 00:43:45.850
And that depends on the
boundary condition, roughness,

00:43:45.850 --> 00:43:47.440
for example, at the boundary.

00:43:47.440 --> 00:43:51.880
We know when light hits the
surface like a table here,

00:43:51.880 --> 00:43:53.950
it's a diffuse scatter.

00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:59.470
If you put a mirror there,
it's a spectral reflected.

00:43:59.470 --> 00:44:01.510
Reality may be
somewhere in between.

00:44:01.510 --> 00:44:04.930
And this is, in fact,
still, nobody really

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:09.100
knows what's happened
at the boundary.

00:44:09.100 --> 00:44:11.740
So the assumption
we'll be doing first

00:44:11.740 --> 00:44:14.630
is a diffuse, so
is isotropically

00:44:14.630 --> 00:44:18.250
scatter in all directions.

00:44:18.250 --> 00:44:20.670
And in this case
conceptually, you

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:25.500
know you have worse
heat condition than when

00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:29.310
you don't have the boundary,
because otherwise the heat will

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:30.730
go from here.

00:44:30.730 --> 00:44:33.240
If you say this is a mean
free path in bulk material,

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:34.870
heat will go from here to here.

00:44:34.870 --> 00:44:37.260
That means at least
the distance was in x

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:39.540
direction is this distance.

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:42.330
But now because of diffuse
scattering, the boundary,

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:45.555
half of them were going
back much earlier.

00:44:48.170 --> 00:44:50.540
So those kind of
size effect typically

00:44:50.540 --> 00:44:54.260
will impede your transport.

00:44:54.260 --> 00:44:58.410
And let's go to say what
the equation tells us.

00:44:58.410 --> 00:45:02.540
I'm going to solve this
equation, the Fourier law.

00:45:02.540 --> 00:45:05.420
And I have an x direction.

00:45:05.420 --> 00:45:10.040
And let's say the x direction
are very long, it's not finite.

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:12.830
But say y direction is finite.

00:45:12.830 --> 00:45:19.970
So we have-- in this case,
I have Vx df dx plus,

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:26.450
let's say here is y
direction, Vy df dy.

00:45:29.390 --> 00:45:35.180
And z direction, I don't have a
gradient, so I can drop that z.

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:38.840
I'm only considering
x direction flow.

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:46.620
So I have minus f minus
f0 steady state over tau.

00:45:46.620 --> 00:45:52.770
This is the equation
you want to solve.

00:45:52.770 --> 00:45:58.350
And I'm going to
again look at the--

00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:00.870
at least to say I
have two direction,

00:46:00.870 --> 00:46:03.900
df dx in the x direction.

00:46:03.900 --> 00:46:14.950
So if I do g equals f minus f0,
now that's a small deviation.

00:46:14.950 --> 00:46:18.690
But this is my difference there.

00:46:18.690 --> 00:46:23.490
And I substitute this
into the equation

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:33.290
here, what I get is Vx
df0 dx, and then dg gx.

00:46:33.290 --> 00:46:36.960
But the x direction, my
variation is relatively small.

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:40.760
I say the x direction
are very long.

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:44.920
So that deviation dg
dx can be dropped out.

00:46:44.920 --> 00:46:52.300
So what I have is Vx df0
dx in the y direction.

00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:58.690
So df0 dx, f0 then
is a function of x.

00:46:58.690 --> 00:47:00.370
I have 1d transport.

00:47:00.370 --> 00:47:04.040
So df0 dy also drops out.

00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:12.370
So what I have is a Vy dg gy
and it equals minus g over tau.

00:47:21.230 --> 00:47:25.250
So this is-- what
I really am looking

00:47:25.250 --> 00:47:31.570
at is the variation because
of the boundary in the y

00:47:31.570 --> 00:47:36.060
direction, I have
variation, size dependence

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:37.480
on the distribution.

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:42.940
So different point, I have
different f in the y direction.

00:47:42.940 --> 00:47:49.110
And so what I'm going to look
at when I rewrite this equation,

00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:59.370
Vy dg dy plus g
over tau and that

00:47:59.370 --> 00:48:08.130
equals negative Vx df0 dT dT dx.

00:48:10.720 --> 00:48:14.370
So you can say if I do not
keep the first term, that's

00:48:14.370 --> 00:48:17.640
essentially what I had before.

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:22.970
I'm only keeping an extra term
due to the constraint in the y

00:48:22.970 --> 00:48:25.880
direction.

00:48:25.880 --> 00:48:31.050
OK, now you can solve this
first order differential

00:48:31.050 --> 00:48:34.220
equation for g.

00:48:34.220 --> 00:48:36.470
And if it's the first
order differentiable

00:48:36.470 --> 00:48:41.123
equation, how many boundary
conditions you need?

00:48:47.750 --> 00:48:48.250
Yeah?

00:48:48.250 --> 00:48:52.990
Somebody can speak up.

00:49:02.053 --> 00:49:05.180
You know It's only one, right?

00:49:05.180 --> 00:49:08.350
You need the only one
boundary condition.

00:49:08.350 --> 00:49:13.230
You need one boundary condition
for any pair of Vx Vy,

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:19.390
because Vx Vy are random,
phase based variable.

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:24.190
So basically what it's
saying is, you see here,

00:49:24.190 --> 00:49:27.580
any direction you look
at, this is the direction

00:49:27.580 --> 00:49:30.830
that they're propagating.

00:49:30.830 --> 00:49:34.260
And you need a boundary
condition in this direction.

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:36.830
And any point, if it's
an internal point,

00:49:36.830 --> 00:49:42.560
I have direction propagating
360 degree, all space,

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:45.360
and this one coming from here.

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:48.080
So I need a boundary condition
either on this surface

00:49:48.080 --> 00:49:51.250
or on this surface.

00:49:51.250 --> 00:49:55.950
And if you can specify one
surface for 360 degree,

00:49:55.950 --> 00:49:59.520
it's a 4 pi solid
angle, you're fine only

00:49:59.520 --> 00:50:01.230
starting with one surface.

00:50:01.230 --> 00:50:04.890
But most of the time, because
I have two constraints,

00:50:04.890 --> 00:50:10.020
and it's better to do for the
one that traveling from leaving

00:50:10.020 --> 00:50:13.320
this surface, I have
boundary condition here

00:50:13.320 --> 00:50:16.100
for this half on bottom.

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:22.050
So that together gives me 4
pi solid space, real space.

00:50:22.050 --> 00:50:24.660
OK so now let's solve this.

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:32.980
And Vy-- so this is
at any point this is y

00:50:32.980 --> 00:50:37.330
direction and the x direction.

00:50:37.330 --> 00:50:43.540
At any point-- let's say this
is a Vy Vx that determine

00:50:43.540 --> 00:50:46.600
the propagation direction.

00:50:46.600 --> 00:50:49.480
And it's again your
choice of whether you

00:50:49.480 --> 00:50:55.570
use a Cartesian coordinate or
use a spherical coordinate.

00:50:55.570 --> 00:50:58.300
And then whether-- if you
use a spherical coordinate,

00:50:58.300 --> 00:51:01.070
which direction you'd say
this is your polar angle,

00:51:01.070 --> 00:51:02.680
which are another angle.

00:51:02.680 --> 00:51:04.860
It's all your freedom.

00:51:04.860 --> 00:51:12.450
And for this problem, I'm going
to do this one as my theta,

00:51:12.450 --> 00:51:18.410
and then say xz plane is
as a [INAUDIBLE] plane.

00:51:18.410 --> 00:51:21.520
So with that, I have--

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:29.160
again, so in this case,
assume V is isotropic.

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:32.680
In real crystal, they
are not isotropic.

00:51:32.680 --> 00:51:34.150
So V is isotropic.

00:51:34.150 --> 00:51:41.144
So I have V cosine theta,
that's the Vy and dg

00:51:41.144 --> 00:51:46.150
dy plus g equals--

00:51:46.150 --> 00:51:53.680
the right hand side is a
term that depends only on x

00:51:53.680 --> 00:51:56.630
Vx, I have to be careful.

00:51:56.630 --> 00:51:59.530
It's dependent on say theta
phi but doesn't depend on y.

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:07.060
So I solve this equation.

00:52:07.060 --> 00:52:09.840
And the way I
solve it-- in fact,

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:16.780
because this is a function
of x, so you say g minus s0 x

00:52:16.780 --> 00:52:17.400
equals--

00:52:17.400 --> 00:52:19.060
it's a homogeneous equation.

00:52:19.060 --> 00:52:23.790
You do a new
variable, g minus s0,

00:52:23.790 --> 00:52:25.630
and then it's V cosine theta.

00:52:25.630 --> 00:52:31.560
So I have a c exponential
minus y V cosine theta.

00:52:37.650 --> 00:52:39.660
That's my solution.

00:52:39.660 --> 00:52:42.716
And you plug in
this back into here.

00:52:42.716 --> 00:52:45.220
You don't believe
it, you can check.

00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:46.000
There should be 0.

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:52.140
So this is my solution.

00:52:52.140 --> 00:52:56.640
And I said that we need
the boundary condition.

00:52:56.640 --> 00:52:57.990
Either y equals 0--

00:53:02.200 --> 00:53:07.940
if y equals 0, the boundary--
if the boundary is diffuse,

00:53:07.940 --> 00:53:12.200
so it goes out isotropically.

00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:14.570
That's corresponding to f0.

00:53:14.570 --> 00:53:18.260
This f going out is equal to f0.

00:53:18.260 --> 00:53:24.810
So g equals 0 at the y equals
0, because f equals f0.

00:53:24.810 --> 00:53:29.340
So g equals 0, y equals
0, so I have c s0.

00:53:29.340 --> 00:53:39.270
