00:56:36.930 --> 00:56:39.795
So I can find out words,
the heat transfer.

00:56:51.830 --> 00:57:01.250
My heat transfer through
any plane of x is--

00:57:01.250 --> 00:57:05.180
this gives me any point
along the y direction.

00:57:05.180 --> 00:57:09.870
So I sum up the flux to
integrate over y direction.

00:57:09.870 --> 00:57:23.230
And integrate over y,
so it's 0 to d over dy.

00:57:23.230 --> 00:57:29.560
And then every flux, I
still have what I had,

00:57:29.560 --> 00:57:34.570
Vx times the h bar
omega and times

00:57:34.570 --> 00:57:39.790
f, that's my say kx ky kz.

00:57:39.790 --> 00:57:42.080
This is the one where
I started when I write

00:57:42.080 --> 00:57:45.040
the flux for all the transport.

00:57:45.040 --> 00:57:47.350
Now because this quantity
is the function y,

00:57:47.350 --> 00:57:49.180
I integrate the y to get total.

00:57:51.690 --> 00:57:55.290
And now you should
be comfortable

00:57:55.290 --> 00:57:59.120
converting the summation
into integration.

00:57:59.120 --> 00:58:05.230
And so what I have,
0 to d and dy.

00:58:05.230 --> 00:58:10.090
And the summation to
integration, what I have is--

00:58:10.090 --> 00:58:17.900
I'm using spherical coordinate
theta phi and V. V is constant,

00:58:17.900 --> 00:58:20.050
is my frequency.

00:58:20.050 --> 00:58:23.330
See I eventually go into
the frequency domain.

00:58:23.330 --> 00:58:35.740
So I have 0 to omega max d
omega And 0 to pi, d phi, and 0

00:58:35.740 --> 00:58:39.130
to pi d theta.

00:58:39.130 --> 00:58:44.680
And the rest is a
Vx h bar omega f.

00:58:47.270 --> 00:58:56.390
This f now is f0 plus g.

00:58:56.390 --> 00:58:59.660
So g for different directions,
I have different solution there.

00:59:01.930 --> 00:59:04.810
I have to split this
0 to pi integration

00:59:04.810 --> 00:59:07.840
into two terms, 0
to 90 degrees, so I

00:59:07.840 --> 00:59:12.792
can use this g, 90 to 180
degrees, I use the bottom g.

00:59:16.900 --> 00:59:19.960
OK, and Vx, what's my Vx?

00:59:23.730 --> 00:59:28.650
My Vx-- so now you see
because I took the easy xy

00:59:28.650 --> 00:59:31.450
direction, theta y direction.

00:59:31.450 --> 00:59:37.480
So I have to project
that V into x direction.

00:59:37.480 --> 00:59:39.940
And that V, if I
project the-- remember

00:59:39.940 --> 00:59:41.410
it's a spherical
coordinate, right?

00:59:41.410 --> 00:59:45.700
Vx is V times sine theta.

00:59:45.700 --> 00:59:49.700
That goes to as a mu
plane, xz direction.

00:59:49.700 --> 00:59:55.550
And the phi, as mu the angle,
if I align to the x direction,

00:59:55.550 --> 00:59:57.800
I have of course phi.

00:59:57.800 --> 01:00:06.050
So this one is V sine
theta cosine phi.

01:00:06.050 --> 01:00:08.660
So this will be x.

01:00:08.660 --> 01:00:14.760
And now when I do solid
angle is theta d theta d

01:00:14.760 --> 01:00:17.940
phi sine theta d theta d phi.

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:22.550
When do from Cartesian
to spherical coordinate,

01:00:22.550 --> 01:00:27.500
I have sine theta d theta d phi.

01:00:27.500 --> 01:00:32.690
And then I have density
of states divide by 4

01:00:32.690 --> 01:00:36.110
pi, because of
density of states was

01:00:36.110 --> 01:00:39.950
for the integrated
whole solid angle.

01:00:43.510 --> 01:00:49.610
So this is the
conversion I have to do.

01:00:49.610 --> 01:00:56.150
And at the end, you see I have
to do all those integrations.

01:00:56.150 --> 01:00:59.330
And this integration you
cannot do if everything depends

01:00:59.330 --> 01:01:04.130
on frequency, relaxation time,
because here s0, I have tau.

01:01:04.130 --> 01:01:06.710
I have another Vx.

01:01:06.710 --> 01:01:11.540
So actually I will
get a Vx squared.

01:01:11.540 --> 01:01:14.690
So here I will have V
squared sine theta squared

01:01:14.690 --> 01:01:15.680
cosine phi squared.

01:01:18.310 --> 01:01:20.920
Otherwise you'll get 0.

01:01:20.920 --> 01:01:25.450
If it's just cosine theta
0 to 2 pi, you get 0.

01:01:25.450 --> 01:01:28.150
But I have a square.

01:01:28.150 --> 01:01:33.790
OK, and if you do
your integration--

01:01:33.790 --> 01:01:38.060
I will skip the detail--

01:01:38.060 --> 01:01:55.460
what you have is here is--

01:01:55.460 --> 01:01:58.430
I want you to really
sit down and make up

01:01:58.430 --> 01:02:01.640
the missing derivations.

01:02:01.640 --> 01:02:09.710
But say I'm going to give
you the result. You will see.

01:02:09.710 --> 01:02:24.180
And if say the tau and
the V are constant,

01:02:24.180 --> 01:02:27.120
or you can say the
tau times the V

01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:31.590
equals mean free
path are constant.

01:02:31.590 --> 01:02:37.050
OK, so here my condition is
mean free path is a constant.

01:02:37.050 --> 01:02:40.720
You go to fluids
problem, when people

01:02:40.720 --> 01:02:46.940
do rarified gas dynamics, they
typically take constant tau.

01:02:46.940 --> 01:02:49.250
You do heat transfer,
people typically

01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:51.940
do constant mean free path.

01:02:51.940 --> 01:02:53.890
I don't see a very
good justification

01:02:53.890 --> 01:02:55.420
for those choices.

01:02:55.420 --> 01:02:58.760
But because of those
different choices,

01:02:58.760 --> 01:03:01.205
the final result
looks like different.

01:03:03.860 --> 01:03:08.420
And so if I do this, and
of course, eventually you

01:03:08.420 --> 01:03:14.180
can write this as your minus
k, but because the integrate,

01:03:14.180 --> 01:03:17.900
so is a d dT dx.

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:22.240
And this is the effective
thermal conductivity.

01:03:22.240 --> 01:03:26.230
And the end result of
all the integration

01:03:26.230 --> 01:03:32.080
is k effective divided
by k, which is the bulk,

01:03:32.080 --> 01:03:41.680
because in the solution,
we have here the df0 dt,

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:46.480
this essentially also gives you
the bulk thermal conductivity.

01:03:46.480 --> 01:03:56.560
So when I normalize it, I have
k effective k bulk equals 1 8 3

01:03:56.560 --> 01:04:05.740
xi 1 minus E3 xi minus E5 xi.

01:04:05.740 --> 01:04:11.950
And this xi is the long
dimensional thickness,

01:04:11.950 --> 01:04:15.520
thickness divided
by mean free path.

01:04:15.520 --> 01:04:23.170
And E is an exponential
function equals

01:04:23.170 --> 01:04:35.140
defined as 0 to 1 mu n minus 2
exponential minus xi over mu d

01:04:35.140 --> 01:04:36.220
mu.

01:04:36.220 --> 01:04:39.010
So this is a function
that in the past

01:04:39.010 --> 01:04:41.910
the people actually turbulate.

01:04:41.910 --> 01:04:47.360
It's an integral
function of order n.

01:04:47.360 --> 01:04:50.780
So this is where I say if you
take a constant relaxation

01:04:50.780 --> 01:04:53.660
time, you're not
getting this En,

01:04:53.660 --> 01:04:58.150
you actually get a
different exponential.

01:04:58.150 --> 01:05:01.920
That's where the difference are.

01:05:01.920 --> 01:05:08.400
OK, so if you plot this
effective thermal conductivity

01:05:08.400 --> 01:05:09.600
of materials.

01:05:09.600 --> 01:05:15.390
This is your xi, which
is d over mean free path.

01:05:15.390 --> 01:05:20.070
And this is a normalized
effective over k bulk,

01:05:20.070 --> 01:05:21.990
let's say bulk.

01:05:21.990 --> 01:05:27.600
And it goes-- so this happens
when this transition goes

01:05:27.600 --> 01:05:29.880
to-- this is of the order 1.

01:05:29.880 --> 01:05:33.450
You approach the bulk
thermal conductivity

01:05:33.450 --> 01:05:37.110
when the material is very thick
compared to mean free path.

01:05:37.110 --> 01:05:41.190
But it's small, you
have the deviation

01:05:41.190 --> 01:05:42.420
from this mean free path.

01:05:42.420 --> 01:05:44.580
So this are the--

01:05:44.580 --> 01:05:48.310
in nanostructure, you
say this property--

01:05:48.310 --> 01:05:50.950
you eventually should
go back to bulk

01:05:50.950 --> 01:05:53.170
when you make it thick enough.

01:05:53.170 --> 01:05:57.580
And then if you have a
theory, you sanity check.

01:05:57.580 --> 01:06:00.850
When you go back to the
bulk, of say thickness

01:06:00.850 --> 01:06:04.940
do you get the bulk properties.

01:06:04.940 --> 01:06:08.680
And so this solution
is also called

01:06:08.680 --> 01:06:10.180
Fuchs-Sondheimer solution.

01:06:19.960 --> 01:06:22.390
Sonderham or Sondheimer--

01:06:22.390 --> 01:06:26.480
OK, now I'm putting solution.

01:06:26.480 --> 01:06:33.860
And it was developed for
electrical transport.

01:06:33.860 --> 01:06:38.030
So the original study was
not on thermal conductivity.

01:06:38.030 --> 01:06:41.750
It was measuring the electrical
conductivity of films

01:06:41.750 --> 01:06:44.420
that turns out to be smaller.

01:06:44.420 --> 01:06:48.350
And the solution is identical
for electrical and thermal

01:06:48.350 --> 01:06:48.890
transport.

01:06:48.890 --> 01:06:53.630
So you can also say this could
be the electric conductivity

01:06:53.630 --> 01:06:56.750
divided by the bulk
electric conductivity,

01:06:56.750 --> 01:07:03.050
and the thickness divided by
bulk electron mean free path.

01:07:03.050 --> 01:07:06.870
You all have the
same behavior there.

01:07:06.870 --> 01:07:10.950
And this is because
the surface scattering.

01:07:10.950 --> 01:07:15.900
But here we are assuming--
this is a comment--

01:07:15.900 --> 01:07:19.170
we're assuming that
they film deposited

01:07:19.170 --> 01:07:21.960
the same as the bulk material.

01:07:21.960 --> 01:07:25.590
And very often this is
actually not the case

01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:27.360
because when you
deposit the film,

01:07:27.360 --> 01:07:32.070
for example, you put a silver
on silicon, what happens

01:07:32.070 --> 01:07:36.330
is it's not a single
crystal, it's grains.

01:07:36.330 --> 01:07:38.310
So in fact, let's
say the nucleation

01:07:38.310 --> 01:07:41.970
when you deposit the material
on top of a substrate,

01:07:41.970 --> 01:07:43.800
first you have a nucleation.

01:07:43.800 --> 01:07:45.300
So the atoms are nucleate.

01:07:45.300 --> 01:07:47.720
They're small dots.

01:07:47.720 --> 01:07:52.160
And then there are a
lot of small crystals.

01:07:52.160 --> 01:07:55.610
And when you further deposit
it, some of the smaller crystals

01:07:55.610 --> 01:07:57.230
disappear.

01:07:57.230 --> 01:07:59.150
The larger crystal win.

01:07:59.150 --> 01:08:03.750
So your crystal grow
larger and larger.

01:08:03.750 --> 01:08:08.970
So the grains actually--
grain size is not a constant.

01:08:08.970 --> 01:08:11.210
And in this case,
what you measure

01:08:11.210 --> 01:08:15.940
is not just the surface
effects, but the interface

01:08:15.940 --> 01:08:18.520
also, because the
grain interface

01:08:18.520 --> 01:08:21.580
are scattering centers.

01:08:21.580 --> 01:08:25.240
So you just can't
always say it's

01:08:25.240 --> 01:08:28.630
this effect that happens at
the surface, interface also.

01:08:32.020 --> 01:08:35.740
And the other comment is if
you have a specular surface,

01:08:35.740 --> 01:08:38.140
you can, again,
solve the equation.

01:08:38.140 --> 01:08:43.560
But the boundary condition
change the boundary condition.

01:08:43.560 --> 01:08:47.390
And in this case, what you see
is that the specular surface

01:08:47.390 --> 01:08:50.050
do not degrade your transport.

01:08:50.050 --> 01:08:50.979
You get what?

01:08:50.979 --> 01:08:54.080
The ratio approach 1.

01:08:54.080 --> 01:08:57.390
And the reason is, if you
think about this, specular,

01:08:57.390 --> 01:09:00.020
even though there is
a direction change,

01:09:00.020 --> 01:09:02.359
but there is still the
same amount of-- so this

01:09:02.359 --> 01:09:05.430
is the original,
goes here but here.

01:09:05.430 --> 01:09:10.010
So in terms of transport in
the x direction, the same.

01:09:10.010 --> 01:09:12.890
There's no effect in scatter.

01:09:12.890 --> 01:09:14.840
So it's like a waveguide.

01:09:14.840 --> 01:09:17.870
You bounce your light
in an optical fiber

01:09:17.870 --> 01:09:21.163
as long as your fiber surface
is specular for light,

01:09:21.163 --> 01:09:21.830
they go forward.

01:09:24.640 --> 01:09:27.279
And there are also solutions
for partially specular,

01:09:27.279 --> 01:09:29.170
partially diffuse.

01:09:29.170 --> 01:09:33.399
So all those are
existing solutions.

01:09:36.359 --> 01:09:41.970
OK, so the original
observation-- those

01:09:41.970 --> 01:09:44.130
are the classical size effects.

01:09:44.130 --> 01:09:49.229
And the first experiment
was done by Casimir.

01:09:49.229 --> 01:09:51.300
First theory was
done by Casimir.

01:09:51.300 --> 01:09:55.350
So Casimir turns out to be--

01:09:55.350 --> 01:09:57.390
there are many Casimir effects.

01:09:57.390 --> 01:10:00.240
And one of those
is heat conduction,

01:10:00.240 --> 01:10:04.200
where he explained this
Casimir factor based on--

01:10:04.200 --> 01:10:08.370
because there are some
experimental diamond.

01:10:08.370 --> 01:10:11.490
I showed before, I commented
before, thermal conductivity

01:10:11.490 --> 01:10:15.990
of bulk material is
always looking like this.

01:10:15.990 --> 01:10:17.940
At the very low
temperature, it's

01:10:17.940 --> 01:10:20.630
proportional to specific heat.

01:10:20.630 --> 01:10:23.690
And that's because there
is no internal scattering.

01:10:23.690 --> 01:10:27.490
The mean free path is
limited by the boundaries.

01:10:27.490 --> 01:10:33.880
So those early experiments,
the size effect was in 1920s.

01:10:33.880 --> 01:10:38.020
And Casimir had a theory, I
think it was in the 1920s--

01:10:38.020 --> 01:10:41.290
and actually I forget
if it was 1920 or 1910

01:10:41.290 --> 01:10:43.750
or 1900, near the range.

01:10:43.750 --> 01:10:47.410
But he was explaining
those effects

01:10:47.410 --> 01:10:49.010
using the boundary scatter.

01:10:49.010 --> 01:10:53.470
So it's also Casimir effect.

01:10:53.470 --> 01:10:55.570
One further comment,
in fact those

01:10:55.570 --> 01:10:57.730
are the things we're writing
on paper on coherence.

01:10:57.730 --> 01:11:01.370
And I was thinking
this weekend where is

01:11:01.370 --> 01:11:05.370
this decoherence coming from?

01:11:05.370 --> 01:11:07.365
So if I have a film--

01:11:10.050 --> 01:11:11.850
we're looking at the
in plane direction.

01:11:11.850 --> 01:11:16.110
But the next one I'll
talk about this direction.

01:11:16.110 --> 01:11:19.980
So if there is no
scattering inside,

01:11:19.980 --> 01:11:24.630
the phonons will propagate
as a wave packet.

01:11:24.630 --> 01:11:26.310
But they don't gather--

01:11:26.310 --> 01:11:28.260
if there is no scattering,
that wave packet

01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:29.340
will propagate as a wave.

01:11:29.340 --> 01:11:32.900
So the phase is
actually maintained.

01:11:32.900 --> 01:11:38.670
So it's a coherent transport
until it hits the surface.

01:11:38.670 --> 01:11:42.300
When it hits the surface,
the Casimir treatment here

01:11:42.300 --> 01:11:46.560
is it completely diffused out.

01:11:46.560 --> 01:11:51.370
So there, you are
phase is randomized.

01:11:51.370 --> 01:11:55.630
And when you randomize
and when you come back,

01:11:55.630 --> 01:11:58.210
again, any of those
phonons that are

01:11:58.210 --> 01:12:01.870
launched from any direction,
that part is still coherent.

01:12:01.870 --> 01:12:04.510
But it's not interfering
with this one

01:12:04.510 --> 01:12:07.060
that going up that
direction because the phases

01:12:07.060 --> 01:12:09.420
are randomized.

01:12:09.420 --> 01:12:17.270
So these are the classical
regime, classical size effect.

01:12:17.270 --> 01:12:21.620
Doesn't mean there's no
phase coherence at all.

01:12:21.620 --> 01:12:27.620
So I think what I'll do next--

01:12:27.620 --> 01:12:30.670
you have heard a
lot now and really

01:12:30.670 --> 01:12:34.750
it's a time to take
a look at the other--

01:12:34.750 --> 01:12:36.492
the recent literature.

01:12:39.390 --> 01:12:42.770
Now you look at it--

01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:44.120
oh, no, not this one.

01:12:49.740 --> 01:12:56.910
I will say that probably if you
look carefully on these papers,

01:12:56.910 --> 01:13:01.950
you should be able to
at least catch something

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:08.800
that we talked in this class
in many, many journals.

